		{"id":"c6220c8a6e4bc606","type":"text","text":"# Week 6 — Writing Session\n\n## IELTS Task 1 Process Diagram: Reusable Sentence Structures\n\n**Level:** B1–B2  \n**Duration:** 2 hours  \n**Main skill:** Process description  \n**Example:** Biofuel production — how ethanol is made  \n**Output:** 1 full Task 1 report trong lớp\n\n## Teaching principle\n\n> See the stage → choose a sentence pattern → plug in the information → connect to the next stage.\n\nKhông dành nhiều thời gian giảng theory.\n\n|Role|Target|\n|---|---|\n|Teacher talk|20–25%|\n|Student analyse / build sentences / write|75–80%|\n\n## Lesson Flow\n\n|Time|Stage|Purpose|\n|---|---|---|\n|0–10 mins|Diagram discovery|Students tự đọc và xếp stages|\n|10–20 mins|Micro input|Structure của Process Task 1|\n|20–40 mins|Sentence pattern drill|Học 10 cấu trúc reusable|\n|40–60 mins|Passive + sequencing drill|Chuyển data thành câu|\n|60–70 mins|Overview challenge|Linear vs cyclical process|\n|70–80 mins|Break||\n|80–95 mins|Guided paragraph building|Viết ethanol process theo nhóm|\n|95–115 mins|Full Task 1 writing|Viết bài hoàn chỉnh|\n|115–120 mins|Fast correction|Chỉ sửa lỗi high-impact|\n\n# 1. First: Students Read the Diagram\n\n## 0–10 mins\n\nKhông giải thích diagram ngay.\n\nCho học viên làm nhóm 2–3 người và trả lời:\n\n1. Where does the process begin?\n2. Where does it end?\n3. Is it linear or cyclical?\n4. Which stages are natural?\n5. Which stages are industrial?\n6. What is the raw material?\n7. What is the final product?\n8. What returns to the beginning of the process?\n\n### Expected flow\n\n> sunlight + CO₂  \n> → plants and trees grow  \n> → harvesting  \n> → pre-processing  \n> → cellulose  \n> → processing  \n> → sugars  \n> → microbes added  \n> → ethanol  \n> → cars / trucks / aircraft  \n> → CO₂  \n> → plants grow again\n\nImportant insight:\n\n> Students should understand the **logic of the process before writing sentences**.\n\n# 2. Process Task 1 Structure\n\n## 10–20 mins\n\nOnly teach this:\n\n### Paragraph 1 — Introduction\n\nWhat does the diagram show?\n\n### Paragraph 2 — Overview\n\n- Linear or cyclical?\n- How many broad phases?\n- Starting point?\n- Final point?\n\n### Paragraph 3 — Early stages\n\nBeginning → middle\n\n### Paragraph 4 — Later stages\n\nMiddle → final stage / return to beginning\n\n## Formula\n\n> **Introduction → Overview → Early stages → Later stages**\n\n# 3. The 12 Reusable Process Sentence Patterns\n\n## 20–40 mins\n\nĐây nên là trọng tâm chính của lesson.\n\n## Pattern 1 — Introduction\n\n> **The diagram illustrates how + product + is produced.**\n\nExample:\n\n> The diagram illustrates how ethanol biofuel is produced from plant material.\n\nReusable:\n\n> The diagram illustrates how coffee is produced.\n\n> The diagram illustrates how waste water is treated.\n\n> The diagram illustrates how electricity is generated from solar energy.\n\n## Pattern 2 — Overview: Linear Process\n\n> **Overall, the process consists of several stages, beginning with X and ending with Y.**\n\nExample:\n\n> Overall, the process consists of several stages, beginning with the collection of raw materials and ending with the finished product.\n\n## Pattern 3 — Overview: Cyclical Process\n\n> **Overall, this is a cyclical process in which X eventually returns to / contributes to the initial stage.**\n\nExample:\n\n> Overall, this is a cyclical process in which carbon dioxide produced at the final stages contributes to the growth of new plants.\n\n## Pattern 4 — Starting the Process\n\n> **The process begins with + noun.**\n\n> **Initially, + subject + verb.**\n\nExample:\n\n> The process begins with the growth of plants and trees.\n\n> Initially, plants absorb sunlight and carbon dioxide.\n\n## Pattern 5 — Passive Voice\n\n> **X is + past participle + before being + past participle.**\n\nExample:\n\n> The plants are harvested before being sent for pre-processing.\n\nVery reusable:\n\n> The beans are collected before being dried.\n\n> The water is filtered before being stored.\n\n## Pattern 6 — Once Something Has Happened\n\n> **Once X has been + V3, it is then + V3.**\n\nExample:\n\n> Once the plants have been harvested, they are then pre-processed.\n\nReusable everywhere.\n\n## Pattern 7 — After + V-ing / Being + V3\n\n> **After being + V3, X is + V3.**\n\nExample:\n\n> After being pre-processed, the plant material is converted into cellulose.\n\nOr:\n\n> After passing through the filter, the water enters a storage tank.\n\n## Pattern 8 — Moving to the Next Stage\n\n> **X is subsequently / then / next + V3.**\n\nExample:\n\n> The cellulose is subsequently processed to produce sugars.\n\nUseful sequencing:\n\n- initially\n- first\n- next\n- then\n- subsequently\n- following this\n- at this stage\n- finally\n\n## Pattern 9 — Producing Something\n\n> **This produces + noun.**\n\n> **This results in + noun.**\n\n> **X is processed to produce Y.**\n\nExample:\n\n> The cellulose is processed to produce sugars.\n\n> This results in a usable source of sugar.\n\n## Pattern 10 — Adding Something\n\n> **X is added to Y in order to + verb.**\n\nOr:\n\n> **X is added to Y, producing Z.**\n\nExample:\n\n> Microbes are added to the sugars, producing ethanol.\n\nThis is excellent for chemical / food / manufacturing processes.\n\n## Pattern 11 — Relative Clause to Connect Two Stages\n\n> **X, which is then + V3, ...**\n\nExample:\n\n> The cellulose is converted into sugar, which is then combined with microbes.\n\nThis helps students avoid writing:\n\n> Then... Then... Then... Then...\n\n## Pattern 12 — Closing a Cycle\n\n> **The cycle then repeats when + clause.**\n\n> **This material is returned to the beginning of the process.**\n\nExample:\n\n> The cycle then repeats when the carbon dioxide is absorbed by growing plants.\n\n# 4. The Most Important Grammar: Passive Voice\n\n## 40–50 mins\n\nProcess Task 1 heavily uses passive because:\n\n> **What happens is more important than who does it.**\n\n## Active\n\n> Machines harvest the plants.\n\n## Passive\n\n> The plants are harvested by machines.\n\nFor IELTS Process, the second sentence is usually more useful.\n\n## Quick Drill\n\nChange these into passive.\n\n### 1\n\nWorkers harvest the plants.\n\n→\n\n> The plants are harvested.\n\n### 2\n\nMachines break down the plant material.\n\n→\n\n> The plant material is broken down.\n\n### 3\n\nFactories process the cellulose.\n\n→\n\n> The cellulose is processed.\n\n### 4\n\nManufacturers add microbes to the sugar.\n\n→\n\n> Microbes are added to the sugar.\n\n### 5\n\nVehicles use ethanol as fuel.\n\n→\n\n> Ethanol is used as fuel by vehicles.\n\n# 5. Avoid Repeating “Then”\n\n## 50–60 mins\n\nWeak writing:\n\n> Then the plants are harvested. Then they are processed. Then cellulose is produced. Then sugar is produced.\n\nUpgrade:\n\n> Once the plants have grown, they are harvested and sent for pre-processing. The resulting material is converted into cellulose, which is subsequently processed to produce sugars.\n\n## Sequencing Bank\n\n|Basic|Better|\n|---|---|\n|First|Initially / At the first stage|\n|Then|Next / Subsequently|\n|After that|Following this|\n|Then|At this stage|\n|Last|Finally / At the final stage|\n\nBut students should understand:\n\n> **Do not put a linker at the beginning of every sentence.**\n\nUse grammar to create flow.\n\n# 6. Sentence Combining Drill\n\nGive students fragments.\n\n### Input\n\n- plants are harvested\n- plants go to pre-processing\n- cellulose is produced\n\n### Weak\n\n> Plants are harvested. Then plants go to pre-processing. Then cellulose is produced.\n\n### Better\n\n> Once the plants have been harvested, they undergo pre-processing, which produces cellulose.\n\n## Another\n\nInput:\n\n- cellulose processed\n- sugars produced\n- microbes added\n- ethanol created\n\n### Better\n\n> The cellulose is processed to produce sugars, to which microbes are then added in order to create ethanol.\n\nFor B1 students, simpler:\n\n> The cellulose is then processed to produce sugars. Microbes are subsequently added to the sugars, producing ethanol.\n\n# 7. Overview Challenge\n\n## 60–70 mins\n\nTeach students that an overview is **not a list of stages**.\n\n## Weak\n\n> Overall, plants grow, they are harvested, then processed, and finally ethanol is produced.\n\nToo detailed.\n\n## Better\n\n> Overall, ethanol production is a cyclical process involving both natural growth and industrial processing. Plant material is converted into fuel, while carbon dioxide released later in the cycle is reused during plant growth.\n\n## Strong reusable overview structures\n\n### Linear\n\n> Overall, the process consists of X main stages, beginning with __________ and culminating in __________.\n\n### Cyclical\n\n> Overall, this is a cyclical process in which __________ is eventually returned to the initial stage.\n\n### Natural + industrial\n\n> Overall, the process involves both natural and man-made stages, starting with __________ before moving through several industrial processes.\n\n# 8. Guided Ethanol Paragraph Building\n\n## 80–95 mins\n\nStudents build the report in groups.\n\n## Paragraph 1 — Introduction\n\nGiven:\n\n> Biofuel production: how ethanol is made\n\nStudents produce:\n\n> The diagram illustrates how ethanol biofuel is produced from plant material.\n\n## Paragraph 2 — Overview\n\nStudents must include:\n\n- cyclical\n- natural + industrial stages\n- plant → fuel → CO₂ → plant\n\nPossible:\n\n> Overall, this is a cyclical process involving both natural growth and industrial production. Plants are converted into ethanol, which is used as fuel, while the carbon dioxide produced later in the process returns to the cycle and supports further plant growth.\n\n## Paragraph 3 — Early stages\n\nKeywords:\n\n> sunlight + CO₂ → plant growth → harvesting → pre-processing → cellulose → sugars\n\nStudents must use at least 3 patterns.\n\nPossible:\n\n> Initially, plants and trees grow by using energy from sunlight together with carbon dioxide. Once mature, they are harvested and sent for pre-processing, during which the plant material is broken down. This produces cellulose, which is subsequently processed to obtain sugars.\n\n## Paragraph 4 — Final stages\n\nKeywords:\n\n> microbes → ethanol → vehicles → CO₂ → cycle repeats\n\nPossible:\n\n> At the next stage, microbes are added to the sugars, producing ethanol. The finished fuel is then used by cars, trucks and aircraft. These vehicles release carbon dioxide, which returns to the atmosphere and is subsequently absorbed by plants, allowing the cycle to begin again.\n\n# 9. Full Writing Task\n\n## 95–115 mins\n\nStudents now write independently.\n\n### Requirement\n\n150–190 words.\n\n### Must include\n\n- Introduction\n- Overview\n- 2 detail paragraphs\n- Passive voice\n- At least 5 sequencing/process structures\n- All major stages\n\n### Cannot use\n\n- personal opinion\n- explanation not shown in diagram\n- “then” more than twice\n- memorised generic openings such as “Nowadays...”\n\n# 10. Strong Model Answer\n\n> The diagram illustrates how ethanol biofuel is produced from plant material and how carbon dioxide is recycled during the process.\n> \n> Overall, ethanol production is a cyclical process involving both natural and industrial stages. Plants are first grown and later converted into fuel, while carbon dioxide released during the later stages returns to the atmosphere and contributes to further plant growth.\n> \n> Initially, plants and trees grow using energy from sunlight and carbon dioxide. Once they are mature, they are harvested and sent for pre-processing, where the plant material is broken down. This produces cellulose, which is subsequently processed in order to obtain sugars.\n> \n> At the next stage, microbes are added to the sugars, producing ethanol. The finished fuel is then used by several forms of transport, including cars, trucks and aircraft. Carbon dioxide is released during this part of the cycle, as well as during pre-processing. This gas is then available for growing plants to absorb, allowing the production cycle to begin again.\n\n# 11. Why This Model Works\n\n|Feature|Example|\n|---|---|\n|Clear paraphrase|ethanol biofuel is produced|\n|Strong overview|cyclical + natural + industrial|\n|Passive voice|are harvested / is processed / are added|\n|Sequencing|initially / once / subsequently / at the next stage|\n|Sentence combining|cellulose, which is subsequently...|\n|Cycle explained|CO₂ → plants → ethanol → CO₂|\n|No speculation|only information shown in diagram|\n\n# 12. High-Value Reusable Process Vocabulary\n\nStudents do **not** need 50 words. Teach these:\n\n## Movement\n\n- enter\n- pass through\n- move to\n- be transferred to\n- be sent to\n\n## Transformation\n\n- be converted into\n- be transformed into\n- be broken down into\n- be processed into\n- produce\n- result in\n\n## Handling\n\n- be collected\n- be harvested\n- be transported\n- be stored\n- be processed\n- be added\n- be heated\n- be cooled\n\n## Final Stage\n\n- finished product\n- final product\n- be distributed\n- be used for\n- be released\n- return to\n- complete the cycle\n\n# 13. Process Grammar Cheat Sheet\n\n|Situation|Structure|\n|---|---|\n|Start|The process begins with...|\n|Passive|X is processed...|\n|Two actions|Once X has been processed, it is...|\n|Sequence|X is subsequently...|\n|Transformation|X is converted into Y.|\n|Production|This produces Y.|\n|Addition|X is added to Y.|\n|Connection|Y, which is then processed,...|\n|Final step|Finally,...|\n|Cycle|The cycle repeats when...|\n\n# 14. Common Errors\n\n|Student Error|Better Version|\n|---|---|\n|The diagram illustrates about...|The diagram illustrates...|\n|Ethanol is manufacture...|Ethanol is manufactured...|\n|Plants are grow.|Plants grow.|\n|Plants are harvested by machine and go process.|Plants are harvested and sent for processing.|\n|Cellulose processes to sugar.|Cellulose is processed to produce sugar.|\n|Microbes add into sugar.|Microbes are added to the sugar.|\n|After harvested, the plants...|After being harvested, the plants...|\n|After the plants harvested...|After the plants are harvested...|\n|It produces to ethanol.|It produces ethanol.|\n|CO₂ releases from cars.|CO₂ is released by cars.|\n|Finally the process is repeat.|Finally, the cycle repeats.|\n\n# 15. Fast Feedback\n\n## 115–120 mins\n\nTeacher only checks five things:\n\n1. **Did you identify linear vs cyclical correctly?**\n2. **Did you write a real overview?**\n3. **Did you include all important stages?**\n4. **Did you use passive voice correctly?**\n5. **Did you connect stages instead of repeatedly writing “then”?**\n\n# 16. Student Final Checklist\n\nBefore submitting:\n\n- Did I paraphrase the diagram?\n- Did I identify whether the process is linear or cyclical?\n- Did I state the beginning and end in the overview?\n- Did I include every major stage?\n- Did I use passive voice?\n- Did I use `Once...`, `After being...`, or `which is then...`?\n- Did I vary my sequencing language?\n- Did I avoid explaining causes that are not shown?\n- Did I avoid personal opinions?\n- Did I write at least 150 words?\n\n# 17. The 8 Sentences Students Should Leave Class Knowing\n\nNếu muốn cực cô đọng, đây là **core memory bank** của buổi học:\n\n1. **The diagram illustrates how X is produced.**\n2. **Overall, the process consists of several stages, beginning with X and ending with Y.**\n3. **The process begins with X.**\n4. **Once X has been processed, it is then transferred to Y.**\n5. **After being processed, X is converted into Y.**\n6. **X is subsequently processed to produce Y.**\n7. **X is added to Y, producing Z.**\n8. **The cycle then repeats when X returns to the initial stage.**\n\nNếu học viên thực sự sử dụng được 8 cấu trúc này, họ đã có bộ khung đủ mạnh để xử lý **food production, recycling, water treatment, manufacturing, agriculture, energy production, cement/bricks, natural cycles và biofuel processes** mà không cần học một template mới cho từng bài.","x":3083,"y":2371,"width":697,"height":16389},
